WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.670
Alright, stepped out of the office for a little bit, new surroundings, and let's take this

2
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:15.750
opportunity for a bit of a recap. ZFS is a physical file system like HFS. It's

3
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:21.390
not an HFS replacement, it's complementary. However, note that HFS has

4
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:27.070
been functionally stabilized since ZOS version 1.6 and new enhancements are only

5
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.630
brought in the context of ZFS. A ZFS aggregate is a data set that contains

6
00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:38.930
the ZFS file system. The aggregate is a VSAM linear data set or VSAM LDS and is

7
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:44.510
a container that can contain one or more ZFS file systems. The name of the aggregate

8
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:50.450
is the same as the VSAM LDS name. A ZFS aggregate that contains only a single

9
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:55.510
ZFS file system can be defined and is called a compatibility mode aggregate.

10
00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.310
Compatibility mode aggregates are more like HFS. Aggregates that contain

11
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:05.270
multiple file systems are called multi-file system aggregates and this

12
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:11.150
type of aggregate is no longer supported. Okay, so you can choose whether each file

13
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:17.390
system is to be an HFS or a ZFS on the data set attributes panel or with the

14
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:25.150
change DSN type command. The root file system can also be a ZFS. VSAM files and

15
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:29.910
ZFSs are not quite as easy to work with as HFSs. For example, you can't

16
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:34.550
uncatalog them and renaming them is a bit more difficult. Although the HFS

17
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.670
data set does not have to be SMS managed it is still highly recommended.

18
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:44.030
Multivolume HFS data sets are only supported as SMS managed that is you

19
00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:49.710
cannot have multivolume non-SMS managed data sets. Finally, you do not

20
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:55.550
need to migrate all of your file systems to ZFS all at once. HFS and ZFS

21
00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:01.430
can coexist and you can migrate some or all at your own pace. Previously you could

22
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:07.270
only migrate mounted HFS file systems to ZFS but now with ZOS 2.1 you can use

23
00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:14.550
the BPXWMIGF command to migrate mounted HFS file systems to ZFS. If you

24
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.150
expect a file system to grow larger than 4 gigabytes and it's not SMS

25
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.430
managed with extended addressability you'll need to copy it to an SMS

26
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.910
managed ZFS data set with a data class that includes extended addressability. If

27
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.350
a ZFS aggregate is to be larger than 4 gigabytes it must be SMS managed with a

28
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:36.910
data class that includes extended addressability. However, there can be

29
00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.230
non-SMS managed VSAM linear data sets that are larger than 4 gigabytes with

30
00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:47.190
the extended addressability feature. Hierarchical file system data sets have

31
00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:52.790
partitioned organization, have the same structure as a PDSE and might or might

32
00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:58.190
not be SMS managed. To create an HFS data set specify the usual DD statement

33
00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:04.270
parameters which are necessary to create any ZOS data set. One way of using a file

34
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:09.510
in the hierarchical file system is to refer to it by JCL. The following

35
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:14.670
changes have been made to support hierarchical files. First off, DSN type

36
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:21.270
equals HFS or pipe. Specify HFS to create a hierarchical file system data set or

37
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:26.950
pipe to specify a named pipe. Path identifies a file in the hierarchical

38
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:32.390
file system. The JCL support allows a path name to be up to 255 characters

39
00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.550
long including the slashes. If the path name contains lower case letters the

40
00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:42.350
name must be enclosed by single quotes. Path DISP specifies the disposition of a

41
00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:48.070
hierarchical file after normal or abnormal end. It can be keep or delete.

42
00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.750
Path mode specifies the file access attributes when the file specified in the

43
00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:57.710
path parameter is created. The file access attributes define who has access

44
00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:03.470
to read, write or execute the file. And path ops specifies the access and

45
00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:08.990
status for a file. There are two file option groups, the access group and the

46
00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:15.390
status group. Now this shows an example of how to allocate an HFS data set for

47
00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:21.870
the root file system. The important JCL keyword here is DSN type equals HFS.

48
00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:27.670
Note that an HFS can be either SMS or non-SMS managed but must be cataloged for

49
00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:32.710
them out to succeed. An alternative to using JCL would be to use the TSO

50
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:40.230
allocate command. The IOE AG FMT utility is used to format an existing VSAM LDS

51
00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:46.550
as a ZFS aggregate. All ZFS aggregates must be formatted before use including

52
00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:52.950
HFS compatibility mode aggregates. IOE AG FMT does not require the ZFS physical

53
00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:57.710
file system to be active on the system. Here is an example of a job to create

54
00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:02.950
and format a compatibility mode aggregate. It's a two-step job. The first

55
00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:07.670
step creates the aggregate and the second step formats it. Since it's a

56
00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:12.390
compatibility mode aggregate, the second step also creates a file system with

57
00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:17.790
the same name as the VSAM LDS cluster entry name and sets the quota to the

58
00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:26.710
size of the aggregate. Just briefly, ZOS 2.1 supports a new directory format V5

59
00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:32.630
using an extensible hashing system. You can optionally migrate to a new version

60
00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:37.670
1.5 file system. There are multiple means to convert to the new file system.

61
00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:43.630
There are new parameters and commands in support of ZFS extended directories. A V5

62
00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:49.350
directory can only exist in a V1.5 aggregate. Version 1.5 aggregates

63
00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:54.630
support extended V5 directories. Extended V5 directories can support larger

64
00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:59.310
directories, store names more efficiently than V4 directories, and reclaim space

65
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:04.430
when names are removed. A V5 directory cannot be created unless a file system

66
00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:10.270
is V5. A file system can only be converted to V5 or created as V5 by

67
00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:18.870
using either the IOE FSUTL convert format or ZFS ADM convert. This JCL

68
00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:23.830
example shows a program in a ZOS data set being relinked to reside in the

69
00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:28.830
hierarchical file system. PATH defines the path name of the directory where the

70
00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:33.790
program payroll resides when linked edited. The path name is in lowercase

71
00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:38.790
letters and therefore enclosed by single quotes. We have some PATH option

72
00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:44.750
options. OWR only specifies the file will be opened. For write, Ocreate

73
00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:48.270
specifies that if the file doesn't exist it's created and if it is

74
00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:53.430
existing it is reused and OTR UNC specifies that the file length will be

75
00:06:53.430 --> 00:07:00.790
truncated to zero if the file already exists. And for PATH mode SIRWXU

76
00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:06.710
specifies that the file owner has read, write and execute permission. SIRWXG

77
00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:12.790
specifies that the file group has read, write and execute permission and SIRWXO

78
00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:18.670
specifies that all other users have read, write and execute permission. Next

79
00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:22.350
we're going to talk about how to get some stuff done in your UNIX

80
00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:28.350
environment using the iShell and directory list utility.
